// Feature: managing people and handing ownership on. Code: routes ((app)/owner; api/v1/users; api/v1/owner/transfer), services/people.
views {
  dynamic view managing_people {
    title 'Managing people'
    description '''
      **The Owner keeps the group's accounts in order without email.**

      **Decisions**
      - The Owner sees every account; admins see the users they invited.
      - A copy has exactly one Owner (decision 80): a partial unique index on auth.users refuses a second one. The
        role list offers only Admin and User ("Campaign Master" and "Player"); the Owner's row has no role list, no
        Disable and no Anonymise, and changing, disabling or anonymising the Owner is refused (409). Ownership moves
        only by Transfer ownership (owner_transfer).
      - Disabling ends every session at once; history stays.
      - A password reset sets a readable temporary password that must be changed at the next sign-in.
      - A disabled account can be anonymised: its display name becomes "Former member".
      - Every change is written to the audit log in the same transaction.
    '''
    owner -> fl.app.web.areas 'change role (Admin or User), disable, reset, anonymise'
    fl.app.web.areas -> fl.app.services.peopleService 'apply'
    fl.app.services.peopleService -> fl.app.repos 'update user (version checked); never the Owner'
    fl.app.services.peopleService -> fl.app.services.authService 'end sessions'
    fl.app.services.peopleService -> fl.app.services.auditService 'record change'
  }

  dynamic view owner_transfer {
    title 'Transfer ownership'
    description '''
      **The Owner hands the copy to another person; there is only ever one Owner, and the old one becomes an Admin.**

      **Decisions**
      - Owner → People has a Transfer ownership panel, for the Owner only: pick an active account other than
        yours, type your password, then confirm in place; the confirm names both people ("<name> becomes the
        Owner. You become a Campaign Master."). POST /api/v1/owner/transfer takes the same { to, password, version }.
      - The password is checked like changing your own (verifyPassword); a wrong one is a field error on password
        and counts towards the sign-in limit (signInLimiter), which answers 429 when it is reached.
      - The target must be another account, active (not disabled, not anonymised), at the row version the form
        showed; otherwise 409. A non-Owner gets 403, an unknown account 404.
      - One transaction locks both rows, makes the Owner an Admin first and then the target the Owner, so
        users_one_owner holds at every moment; it records owner.transferred { from, to }.
      - Nobody is signed out: the role is read on every request. The old Owner lands on /admin with a note naming
        the new Owner. The new Owner can hand it on; the recovery script stays the way back in.
      - Upgrading a copy with several Owners keeps the oldest active one and makes the others Admins, audited as
        role.changed with reason single_owner (migration 0012).
    '''
    owner -> fl.app.web.areas 'Owner → People: Transfer ownership (new Owner, password, confirm in place)'
    fl.app.web.areas -> fl.app.services.peopleService 'transferOwnership { to, password, version }'
    fl.app.services.peopleService -> fl.app.services.authService 'sign-in limiter, verify the password'
    fl.app.services.peopleService -> fl.app.repos 'lock both rows; the Owner to admin, then the target to owner'
    fl.app.repos -> fl.app.authTables 'users_one_owner: never two Owners'
    fl.app.services.peopleService -> fl.app.services.auditService 'record owner.transferred'
    fl.app.web.areas -> owner '303 to /admin with the note'
  }
}
